Put spaces around symbolic operators:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    total = left + right
    count += 1
]

Use words for equality and logic:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    same = left is right
    ready = loaded and valid
    retry = timed_out or disconnected
    blocked = not ready
]

`/` performs real division and produces `F64` for fixed integer operands.
`//` performs integer division in the selected common integer type:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    real_result = 5 / 2
    whole_result = 5 // 2
]

Fixed integer operations preserve an equal operand type and use the wider
operand when signedness matches. Mixed signedness selects the smallest signed
type that holds both complete input ranges:

[table:
    [th: [: Operands] [: Result type]]
    [td: [: I8 + I8] [: I8]]
    [td: [: U8 + U8] [: U8]]
    [td: [: I8 + U8] [: I16]]
    [td: [: I8 + U16] [: I32]]
    [td: [: I64 + U64] [: invalid]]
]

No 32-bit minimum applies. Narrow operations can overflow in their selected
result type. Fixed floats also preserve equal precision, so `F16 + F16` stays
`F16` and rounds after that operation. Unary minus preserves signed integer
and binary-float types. It rejects every unsigned type, including known zero.

`I64` and `U64` still compare exactly even though they have no common
arithmetic type. `Byte` has no arithmetic. `Dec` keeps its scale and
per-operation rounding rules; its exponent type is `I32`.

Compound assignment uses the ordinary arithmetic domain and checks any
required conversion back to the target. It evaluates the place and right
operand once and writes only after success. A catch on the right-hand
expression does not catch later update checks.

Use templates to join text. `+` adds numbers only. See
@./#operators (Operators) for the complete domain table and
@./#checked-arithmetic (Checked arithmetic) for failure delivery and
implementation support.
